---
import DocLink from '@/components/DocLink.astro';
import MotionPage from '@/components/MotionPage.astro';
import ForFramework from '@/components/ForFramework.astro';
import MotionStage from '@/components/motion/MotionStage.astro';
import MotionUsage from '@/components/motion/MotionUsage.astro';
import MotionOptions from '@/components/motion/MotionOptions.astro';
import MotionTokens from '@/components/motion/MotionTokens.astro';
import GoodToKnow from '@/components/motion/GoodToKnow.astro';
import Fact from '@/components/motion/Fact.astro';
import Policy from '@/components/motion/scenes/Policy.astro';
import { PolicyControls } from '@/components/motion/scenes/PolicyControls';
import { glossaryHref } from '@/lib/motion-glossary';
---
<MotionPage
  slug="accessibility"
  title="Motion: reduced motion and accessibility"
  description="The reduced-motion policy of @udixio/motion, what each verb shows under it, and the focus, pause and reading rules that the runtime keeps."
>
  <h2 id="what-each-verb-shows">What each verb shows under reduced motion</h2>
  <p>Under reduced motion, or with <code>--udx-motion: none</code> on the element or an ancestor, each part of the library shows the following.</p>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Verb</th><th>Under reduced motion</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>appear</code>, <code>disappear</code></td><td>A short opacity fade. An exit with <code>exit: 'out-of-flow'</code> is in place.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>emphasize</code></td><td>Not animated: the element applies its end frame.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>reveal</code></td><td>The element is shown at once, unless its definition sets another <code>reduced</code> value.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>scrub</code></td><td>The element stays still; the scroll link is stopped.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>loop</code></td><td>The loop does not play: the element keeps its stylesheet state.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>layout</code></td><td>The new layout applies at once.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Gestures</td><td>The release is written at once, with no glide.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Text</td><td>The text is not split: the element plays whole.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Values</td><td>A smoothed value is written at once.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    <code>--udx-motion: none</code> on an element or an ancestor holds its end state, whatever the <DocLink href={glossaryHref('policy')}>policy</DocLink>.
    Use it per breakpoint with Tailwind: <code>md:motion-none</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Multiplying a transition duration by <code>--udx-motion-scale</code> alone does not make every effect safe. Adapt or remove
    translation and loops, and keep the essential content available. A raw <DocLink href={glossaryHref('raw provider')}>provider</DocLink>, which plays keyframes
    and nothing else, does not apply this policy.
  </p>

  <h2 id="follow-the-policy">Follow the reduced-motion policy</h2>
  <p>
    The runtime follows the reduced-motion policy of the document. The policy is reduced when the system asks for it, with
    <code>prefers-reduced-motion: reduce</code>, or when <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> has <code>data-motion-policy="reduce"</code>. An
    absent attribute, <code>auto</code>, or an unknown value follows the system.
  </p>
  <p>
    The document stylesheet defines <code>--udx-motion-scale</code>: 1 by default, and 0 under the reduced policy. The Reduce motion
    button of the navigation rail writes the attribute for the whole documentation site. The stage below reads the scale, and says what
    it changes.
  </p>
  <MotionStage scene="policy" label="Live example: a card, a pill and a loop under the reduced-motion policy" height={[256, 272]}>
    <Fragment slot="controls"><PolicyControls client:visible /></Fragment>
    <Policy />
  </MotionStage>

  <h2 id="keep-an-element-still">Keep an element still</h2>
  <p>
    An element can keep its end state whatever the policy. Set <code>--udx-motion: none</code> on it, or on an ancestor. With Tailwind,
    the class <code>motion-none</code> does it, and <code>md:motion-none</code> does it from a breakpoint up.
  </p>

  <h2 id="give-focus-back-when-the-element-leaves">Give focus back when the element leaves</h2>
  <p>
    When an element leaves with focus inside it, such as a panel closed by its own Close button, the browser drops focus once the
    element turns <code>inert</code>. Name where focus goes, and the exit moves it there first (WCAG 2.4.3).
  </p>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="html">
      Set <code>data-motion-restore-focus</code> to a CSS selector. It is looked up when the exit starts, and it must match an element
      outside the one that leaves.
    </p>
    <p slot="react">Pass <code>restoreFocus</code> to <code>&lt;Motion&gt;</code>: an element, or a function that returns one when the exit starts.</p>
    <p slot="angular">Bind <code>[udxMotionRestoreFocus]</code> to an element, or to a function that returns one when the exit starts.</p>
    <p slot="svelte">Pass <code>restoreFocus</code> in the options of <code>motion()</code>: an element, or a function that returns one when the exit starts.</p>
  </ForFramework>
  <p>
    The example below keeps a card's presence, sends its focus to the toggle when the card leaves, and shows an element that stays still.
  </p>
  <MotionUsage name="motion-policy" live={false} />

  <h2 id="pause-a-loop">Pause a loop</h2>
  <p>
    A loop never ends, so its definition says how it is paused. Use <code>pause: 'control'</code> when the page offers a control that
    pauses it. Use <code>pause: 'essential'</code>, with a <code>reason</code>, when the loop is the content itself. See
    <DocLink href="/motion/loop">Loop</DocLink>.
  </p>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="html">Set <code>data-motion-paused</code> on the element to pause its loop. Remove the attribute to play the loop again.</p>
    <p slot="react">Pass <code>paused</code> to <code>&lt;Motion&gt;</code>: the loop pauses while it is <code>true</code>, and plays again when it is <code>false</code>.</p>
    <p slot="angular">Bind <code>[udxMotionPaused]</code> to a value: the loop pauses while it is <code>true</code>, and plays again when it is <code>false</code>.</p>
    <p slot="svelte">Pass <code>paused</code> in the options of <code>motion()</code>: the loop pauses while it is <code>true</code>, and plays again when it is <code>false</code>.</p>
  </ForFramework>

  <h2 id="keep-the-content-readable-and-usable">Keep the content readable and usable</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A counter holds its number in an <code>aria-hidden</code> span while it counts, and its final number in a visually hidden span, so that a screen reader reads the final number.</li>
    <li>A split text keeps its semantics. Its visual copy is <code>aria-hidden</code>, so the text is read once, as written: see <DocLink href="/motion/text">Text</DocLink>.</li>
    <li>Every gesture has a keyboard alternative, and a reorder announces each step in a live region: see <DocLink href="/motion/gestures">Gestures</DocLink>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="options">Options</h2>
  <p>
    The policy has three settings, each in its own place: the option <code>reduced</code> in a definition, the CSS variable
    <code>--udx-motion</code> on an element, and the attribute <code>data-motion-policy</code> on the document.
  </p>
  <h3 id="in-a-definition">In a definition: <code>reduced</code></h3>
  <MotionOptions
    caption="The option reduced, in a definition"
    rows={[
      { name: 'reduced', values: 'fade · instant · static · neutral · frozen', default: 'by verb: fade, instant, static, neutral or frozen', description: 'What the motion shows under reduced motion, named in the definition.' },
    ]}
  />
  <h3 id="on-an-element">On an element: <code>--udx-motion</code></h3>
  <MotionOptions
    caption="The CSS variable --udx-motion, on an element"
    rows={[
      { name: '--udx-motion', values: 'none · a defined motion of the same kind', default: 'the motion of the element', description: 'The motion an element plays in place of its own. none holds the end state, whatever the policy.' },
    ]}
  />
  <h3 id="on-the-document">On the document: <code>data-motion-policy</code></h3>
  <MotionOptions
    caption="The attribute data-motion-policy, on the document"
    rows={[
      { name: 'data-motion-policy', values: 'reduce · auto', default: 'auto', description: 'On html: reduce selects the reduced policy; auto, an absent attribute or an unknown value follows the system.' },
    ]}
  />

  <h2 id="good-to-know">Good to know</h2>
  <GoodToKnow>
    <Fact label="Timing">
      <p>
        Without the policy, an entry moves on the default spatial spring and fades on the default effects spring. Under the policy, an entry
        becomes a short fade on the default effects spring.
      </p>
      <MotionTokens tokens={['spring.default.spatial', 'spring.default.effects']} />
    </Fact>
    <Fact label="Limits">
      <ul>
        <li>If an element sits inside a shadow root, import the document stylesheet: the attribute alone does not reach it.</li>
        <li>If a local override sets <code>--udx-motion-scale</code> inside a shadow root, it replaces the inherited 0: avoid such an override where reduction must be enforced.</li>
        <li>If you use a raw provider, apply the policy yourself: the policy applies to the runtime and its adapters only.</li>
      </ul>
    </Fact>
  </GoodToKnow>
</MotionPage>
